# Environment: Claude Code native on Termux/Android

_This section is installed and kept in sync by [claude-code-termux-native](https://github.com/jronminh/claude_code_termux_native)'s `install.sh`. A future install/update overwrites everything between the `claude-code-termux-native:begin`/`:end` markers, so don't hand-edit inside them — fix `scripts/*.sh` or this template in that repo instead, then re-run `install.sh`. Notes that don't belong there (drift you've observed but haven't turned into a proper fix yet) belong outside the markers, elsewhere in this file._

## Rule 0 — Verify yourself, don't treat this file as scripture
This section is a snapshot taken at install time. Paths, versions, and
structure may have changed after any Termux upgrade, glibc upgrade, or
claude update itself. BEFORE concluding something is broken or deciding
what to fix, run `~/.claude/claude-native/doctor.sh` (or the probe
commands below) and trust the actual result more than the words here. If
reality disagrees with this description, reality wins.

Minimum probe commands:
    uname -m                         # expected: aarch64
    echo "$PREFIX"; echo "$HOME"
    command -v claude; readlink -f "$(command -v claude)"
    file ~/.claude/claude-native/claude
    patchelf --print-interpreter ~/.claude/claude-native/claude
    ls -l "$PREFIX/glibc/lib/ld-linux-aarch64.so.1"
    env | grep -i '^LD_'

## Context — where you are actually running
You are NOT running on a regular Linux system. You are Anthropic's official
linux-arm64 glibc binary, with its ELF interpreter patched so it runs on
Android (Bionic libc) via Termux's glibc-runner. Anthropic does not publish
an android-arm64 build; this is a community workaround, not official support.
Consequence: `process.platform` may report `linux` or `android` — don't
branch any important decision on that alone. Verify with real commands.

## Current install state (verify again, don't trust the text)
- Binary:      ~/.claude/claude-native/claude   (patched via patchelf --set-interpreter)
- Interpreter: $PREFIX/glibc/lib/ld-linux-aarch64.so.1
- Wrapper:     $PREFIX/bin/claude       (what actually runs when you type `claude`)
- The self-repair/update kit (binary, autocheck.sh, update.sh, doctor.sh,
  manifest.json, the lock/history files) lives at `~/.claude/claude-native/`.
- glibc libraries are supplied at runtime NOT via the LD_LIBRARY_PATH
  environment variable, but via the `--library-path` flag invoking the
  dynamic linker directly (see trap #1 for why — an env var is inherited by
  child processes, a command-line flag is not). Do not embed an rpath in the
  binary (the bun binary has an appended payload; rewriting section headers
  corrupts it).

The actual wrapper ($PREFIX/bin/claude) currently reads:
    #!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/bash
    unset LD_PRELOAD
    export TMPDIR="$HOME/.cache/claude-tmp"
    mkdir -p "$TMPDIR"
    export USE_BUILTIN_RIPGREP=0
    export DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER=1
    export CLAUDE_CODE_EXECPATH="$HOME/.claude/claude-native/claude"
    exec "$PREFIX/glibc/lib/ld-linux-aarch64.so.1" \
      --library-path "$PREFIX/glibc/lib" \
      "$HOME/.claude/claude-native/claude" "$@"

Every line has a load-bearing reason:
- `unset LD_PRELOAD` — Termux preloads libtermux-exec (Bionic) by default.
  The glibc loader chokes on it with "invalid ELF header" and claude never
  starts.
- Invoking `ld-linux-aarch64.so.1 --library-path ...` instead of setting the
  `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` env var — if set via env (even via `env VAR=val exec ...`),
  this variable LEAKS into EVERY child process claude spawns afterward (bash
  for the Bash tool, rg, etc.), causing Bionic bash to die with "bad ELF
  magic" (see trap #1). Invoking the linker with a command-line flag only
  applies to that one exec, and never leaks into the environment.
- A writable `TMPDIR` — Android has no /tmp.
- `USE_BUILTIN_RIPGREP=0` — use Termux's own rg.
- `DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER=1` — blocks the built-in updater; see the
  future-proofing section.
- `CLAUDE_CODE_EXECPATH="$HOME/.claude/claude-native/claude"` — see trap #8. Without
  this, claude self-detects its execPath as the ld-linux interpreter (the
  last thing actually execve'd), which breaks the built-in `grep` shell
  function it injects into every Bash-tool shell.

## Traps encountered — symptom → cause → fix
1. Termux commands (mkdir, ls, and ESPECIALLY the bash that claude's own Bash
   tool spawns) die with "bad ELF magic: 2f2a2047" or
   "CANNOT LINK EXECUTABLE ... bash"
   → glibc's LD_LIBRARY_PATH was set via an environment variable (even the
     `env LD_LIBRARY_PATH=... exec claude` form), so it LEAKS into every
     child process claude spawns afterward — including the Bionic bash that
     claude itself invokes for the Bash tool. The Bionic linker loads
     glibc's libc.so by mistake and chokes.
   → Root fix: don't set LD_LIBRARY_PATH via env, invoke the dynamic linker
     directly with `--library-path`:
         exec "$PREFIX/glibc/lib/ld-linux-aarch64.so.1" \
           --library-path "$PREFIX/glibc/lib" \
           "$HOME/.claude/claude-native/claude" "$@"
     The command-line flag only applies to that one exec and never leaks
     into the environment for child processes to inherit. After fixing this,
     you MUST fully quit the currently running claude session (it already
     inherited the old env) and open a new one for the fix to take effect.
2. "Permission denied" / "cannot execute: Success" when running the binary
   → binary is missing the execute bit after curl/patchelf. `chmod +x`.
     (The "Success" line is just a stale errno being misprinted — ignore it.)
3. grep/rg dies with "invalid ELF header" mid-session
   → Bionic's LD_PRELOAD leaked into a glibc process. Make sure
     `unset LD_PRELOAD`. Never write env.LD_PRELOAD into
     ~/.claude/settings.json.
4. "cannot execute" / interpreter not found, or it points at
   /lib/ld-linux-aarch64.so.1
   → binary was never patched, OR an update overwrote it with an unpatched
     build. Run patchelf --set-interpreter again, then chmod +x. (autocheck.sh
     does this automatically — see "Self-check + self-heal" below.)
5. claude exits immediately with "native binary not installed"
   → hit the newer binary-distribution mechanism that has no android target.
     You need a patched linux-arm64 build, not one installed via
     npm/claude install.
6. Segfault right after patchelf
   → some glibc binaries won't tolerate patchelf. Fallback: don't patch it,
     run it via `grun <binary>` instead (grun unsets LD_PRELOAD and sets the
     library path itself).
7. Write errors to /tmp / EACCES at runtime
   → TMPDIR isn't set or doesn't exist. Create it and point TMPDIR at a
     writable location.
8. Bare `grep` inside a Bash tool call fails with
   "-G: error while loading shared libraries: -G: cannot open shared
   object file" (real `command grep` and `rg` still work fine)
   → Claude Code injects an exported bash function named `grep` into every
     shell it spawns, which shadows real grep for most flag combos and
     instead does `exec -a ugrep "$CLAUDE_CODE_EXECPATH" -G --ignore-files
     ... "$@"` (a hidden ripgrep-like personality baked into the claude
     binary itself, dispatched via argv[0]=ugrep). If `CLAUDE_CODE_EXECPATH`
     ever points at the ld-linux interpreter instead of the claude binary
     (e.g. a hand-edited wrapper that lost the export), the function ends up
     running the raw linker with grep-style flags, which it can't parse.
   → Root fix (already applied by the installed wrapper above): export
     `CLAUDE_CODE_EXECPATH` to the real binary path directly. This works
     because the raw patched ELF can be exec'd *directly* (confirmed:
     `~/.claude/claude-native/claude --version` runs fine standalone) — this
     glibc's `ld.so.cache` already lists `$PREFIX/glibc/lib` as a system
     search path, so no `--library-path` flag is needed for a direct
     execve of the binary. And because it's a real ELF (not a script),
     `exec -a ugrep <binary> ...` preserves the custom argv[0] all the way
     through the kernel's automatic PT_INTERP dispatch to ld.so — unlike
     routing through the wrapper *script*, where the kernel's shebang
     handling discards any custom argv[0] before bash ever sees it.
   → As with trap #1, a currently-running claude session already inherited
     whatever `CLAUDE_CODE_EXECPATH` was set to at startup — fully quit and
     reopen for any fix here to take effect.

## Future-proofing
- DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER=1 must always be present in BOTH places: exported by
  the wrapper ($PREFIX/bin/claude) AND set in ~/.claude/settings.json. The
  in-process updater is more stubborn than it looks: blocking one layer
  still lets it download an unpatched build on top of the patched one →
  claude dies next launch. ~/.claude/claude-native/autocheck.sh
  automatically re-adds the settings.json key if it finds it missing (see
  "Self-check + self-heal" below); `doctor.sh` checks both places.
- Updating is NOT a manual step by default — see "Self-check + self-heal"
  below. ~/.claude/claude-native/update.sh --check-only runs automatically
  every time Termux opens and just reports whether a new version exists;
  `termux-update-claude` (no flag) does the actual download/verify/patch.
  You cannot hot-swap the binary of a currently running session — quit and
  reopen to pick up a new build.
- After EVERY Termux `pkg upgrade` or glibc bump: run doctor.sh. If the
  loader path changed or the glibc version jumped, patchelf
  --print-interpreter may still match while the actual library underneath
  has drifted — re-patch to be safe.
- The manifest.json structure isn't guaranteed stable. The arm64 checksum
  key is currently inferred to be `.platforms["linux-arm64"].checksum` but
  this has NOT been confirmed with certainty. If update.sh reads it as
  null/empty, it stops itself and writes a full report — open that report,
  or open manifest.json yourself to see the real structure and fix the key
  in the repo's `scripts/update.sh`.

## Self-check + self-heal (autocheck.sh, runs every time Termux opens)
- ~/.bashrc sources `~/.claude/claude-native/autocheck.sh` on every
  interactive shell. This script ONLY runs when actually opening Termux (a
  login shell reading .bashrc) — it does NOT run inside the bash that
  claude itself spawns for the Bash tool (non-interactive, doesn't read
  .bashrc), so it causes no slowdown or side effects inside a claude
  session.
- autocheck.sh self-heals (silent if there's nothing to fix):
    - defensively unsets LD_PRELOAD/LD_LIBRARY_PATH, mkdir's TMPDIR
    - chmod +x's the binary back if it lost its execute bit
    - re-runs patchelf on the interpreter if the binary was overwritten by
      an unpatched build (typically by the autoupdater) — no re-download
      needed, patches in place
    - re-adds DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER=1 to settings.json if it went missing
    - warns (does NOT auto-fix) if the wrapper was changed back to setting
      LD_LIBRARY_PATH via an environment variable (trap #1 recurring) — this
      case must be fixed by hand, since auto-editing an executable wrapper
      script carries more risk than benefit
  It then calls `update.sh --check-only` — CHECK ONLY, it never
  auto-downloads or installs anything, so opening Termux gets you a usable
  prompt immediately with no waiting on the network.

- **Locking**: the chmod/patchelf/settings-edit block above runs inside a
  `flock` on `~/.claude/claude-native/.claude-native.lock`. update.sh's own
  install step takes the SAME lock file, so if two Termux tabs are open at
  once, one self-healing the binary while the other is mid-update, they
  can't stomp on each other: whichever loses the race just waits a few
  seconds, then backs off with a message instead of corrupting the file.

- **Repatch-frequency escalation**: every time autocheck.sh has to re-patch
  the interpreter, it appends a timestamp to
  `~/.claude/claude-native/.repatch-history` (trimmed to the last 50
  entries). If 2 or more re-patches have happened within the last 24h, it
  escalates from a quiet one-line fix notice to an explicit WARNING that
  DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER may not actually be holding.

- **Rollback**: before installing a new version, update.sh copies the
  current binary/manifest to `claude.prev` / `manifest.json.prev`.
  `termux-update-claude --rollback` swaps `claude.prev` back into place
  (keeping the rejected new build aside as `claude.rejected` instead of
  deleting it) and refuses cleanly with a clear message if no backup
  exists.

- **termux-update-claude** ($PREFIX/bin/termux-update-claude): the
  dedicated command for the user to type by hand. Serves both "manual
  check" and "confirm the update" — typing this command IS the
  confirmation, no y/n prompt on top of it. Running it with no new version
  available reports "Already on the latest version"; if there is a new
  version, it downloads (showing live progress — a big binary on a slow
  mobile link can take minutes) and installs right away, with automatic
  retry/resume (`curl --retry --retry-all-errors -C -`) if the connection
  drops mid-transfer. `termux-update-claude --rollback` reverts to the
  previous binary.

- update.sh has 2 modes, distinguished by the `--check-only` flag:
    - **`update.sh --check-only`** (called by autocheck.sh at Termux
      startup): a short-timeout version check, compared against the
      currently installed binary. Same version, or any failure (no
      network, etc.) → SILENT, exits immediately — this is an automatic
      check on every Termux launch, not a deliberate user action. New
      version available → prints exactly one line pointing at
      `termux-update-claude`, never downloads anything itself.
    - **`update.sh`** (no flag): does the real work — downloads binary +
      manifest.json, verifies SHA-256, patches, and only moves the new
      binary into place after every verification step has passed. Any
      step failing prints a full diagnostic report to the screen and saves
      it to `~/.claude/claude-native/update-fail-<timestamp>.log`.

## Golden rules for self-repair
- Always distinguish: is this command Bionic (Termux) or glibc (you)? Don't
  let a glibc env leak into a Bionic command, or vice versa.
- Read the real errno/message, don't get led astray by noise lines like
  "Success".
- When stuck, grun is the safe fallback.
- After fixing something: rerun `~/.claude/claude-native/doctor.sh`. If
  what you found is a real, reproducible gap in this setup (not specific to
  this one machine), it belongs as a fix in
  [claude-code-termux-native](https://github.com/jronminh/claude_code_termux_native)
  — a PR against its `scripts/*.sh` and this template helps the next
  install too.
